To solve the given system of equations for $y$:
\begin{align*}
4x-y+2z&=7 \quad (1)\\
6x+3y+3z&=5 \quad (2)
\end{align*}

We want to eliminate both $x$ and $z$ to find the value of $y$. We can do this by finding a linear combination of the two equations. Let's multiply equation (1) by 3 and equation (2) by 2:
\begin{align*}
3(4x - y + 2z) &= 3(7) \implies 12x - 3y + 6z = 21 \quad (3)\\
2(6x + 3y + 3z) &= 2(5) \implies 12x + 6y + 6z = 10 \quad (4)
\end{align*}

Now, we subtract equation (3) from equation (4):
\begin{align*}
(12x + 6y + 6z) - (12x - 3y + 6z) &= 10 - 21 \\
12x - 12x + 6y - (-3y) + 6z - 6z &= -11 \\
9y &= -11
\end{align*}

Solving for $y$, we get:
$$y = -\frac{11}{9}$$

To verify, let's check if the direction vector of the intersection line has a zero $y$-component. The normal vectors to the planes are $\mathbf{n}_1 = (4, -1, 2)$ and $\mathbf{n}_2 = (6, 3, 3)$. The direction vector $\mathbf{v}$ is:
$$\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{n}_1 \times \mathbf{n}_2 = \begin{vmatrix} \mathbf{i} & \mathbf{j} & \mathbf{k} \\ 4 & -1 & 2 \\ 6 & 3 & 3 \end{vmatrix}$$
$$\mathbf{v} = \mathbf{i}(-3 - 6) - \mathbf{j}(12 - 12) + \mathbf{k}(12 - (-6)) = -9\mathbf{i} - 0\mathbf{j} + 18\mathbf{k}$$
Since the $y$-component of the direction vector is 0, $y$ remains constant along the line. We already found this constant value to be $-\frac{11}{9}$.

